==English==
{{was wotd|2016|March|30}}
{{wp|<dab>}}

===Etymology 1===
{{word|en|ine|h₂ékʷeh₂}}
[[File:Orfice.jpg|Inside an underground sewer (etymology 1)|thumb|right]]
From {{inh|en|enm|sewer}}, {{m|enm|seuer}}, from {{uder|en|xno|sewere||water-course}}, from {{uder|en|fro|sewiere||overflow channel for a fishpond}}, from {{uder|en|la-vul|*exaquāria||drain for carrying water off}}, from {{uder|en|la|ex||out of, from}} + {{m|la|aquaria|aquāria|of or pertaining to waters}} or from a root {{m|la|*exaquāre}}.

====Pronunciation====
* {{enPR|so͞o′ə|a=RP}}, {{IPA|en|/ˈs(j)uːə/}}
* {{enPR|so͞o′ər|a=GA}}, {{IPA|en|/ˈsuɚ/}}
* {{audio|en|en-uk-sewer.ogg|a=UK|IPA=/ˈsjuːə/, /ˈsuːə/}}
* {{audio|en|en-us-sewer.ogg|a=US}}
* {{audio|en|en-au-sewer.ogg|a=AU}}
* {{homophones|en|suer}}<!--one who sues-->
* {{rhymes|en|uːə(ɹ)|s=2}}
* {{hyphenation|en|sew|er}}

====Noun====
{{wp}}
{{en-noun}}

# A [[pipe]] or channel, or system of pipes or channels, used to remove human [[waste]] and to provide [[drainage]].
#: {{ux|en|There was a blockage in the '''sewer''' after an item of clothing was flushed down the toilet.}}
#* {{RQ:Economist|title=It's a gas|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20140612194203/http://www.economist.com/news/science-and-technology/21604091-it-possible-sniff-out-problems-sewer-pipes-they-happen-its-gas|date=14 June 2014|volume=411|issue=8891|passage=One of the hidden glories of Victorian engineering is proper drains. Isolating a city’s effluent and shipping it away in underground '''sewers''' has probably saved more lives than any medical procedure except vaccination.}}

